.TH "FLOX\-INSTALL" "1" "" "" "Flox User Manuals"
.SH NAME
flox\-install \- install packages to an environment
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IP
.EX
flox [<general options>] install
     [\-i <id>] <package>[\(at<version>]
     [\-i <id>] <package>[\(ha<outputs>]
     [[\-i <id>] <package>] ...
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
Install packages to an environment.
.PP
Package installation is transactional.
During an installation attempt the environment is built in order to
validate that the environment isn\(cqt broken (for example, in rare
cases packages may provide files that conflict).
If building the environment fails, including any of the constituent
packages, the attempt is discarded and the environment is unmodified.
If the build succeeds, the environment is atomically updated.
.PP
If a requested package is already installed, nothing is done.
If multiple packages are requested and some of them are already
installed, only the new packages are installed and the transaction will
still succeed as long as the build succeeds.
.PP
You may also specify packages to be installed via
\f[B]\f[CB]flox\-edit(1)\f[B]\f[R], which allows specifying a variety of
options for package installation.
See \f[B]\f[CB]manifest.toml(5)\f[B]\f[R] for more details on the
available options.
.SS Install ID
The name of a package as it exists in the manifest is referred to as the
\(lqinstall ID\(rq.
This ID is separate from the pkg\-path and provides a shorthand for
packages with long names such as \f[CR]python310Packages.pip\f[R].
Install IDs also provide a way to give packages more semantically
meaningful, convenient, or aesthetically pleasing names in the manifest
(e.g.\ \f[CR]node21\f[R] instead of \f[CR]nodejs_21\f[R]).
When not explicitly provided, the install ID is inferred based on the
pkg\-path.
For pkg\-paths that consist of a single attribute
(e.g.\ \f[CR]ripgrep\f[R]) the install ID is set to that attribute.
For pkg\-paths that consist of multiple attributes
(e.g.\ \f[CR]python310Packages.pip\f[R]) the install ID is set to the
last attribute in the pkg\-path (e.g.\ \f[CR]pip\f[R]).
.PP
As an advanced feature, a Nix flake installable may be specified instead
of a pkg\-path, and in this case the install ID is inferred from the
attribute path specified, or if no attribute path is provided, the
install ID is inferred from the flake reference.
.SS Package names
Packages are organized in a hierarchical structure such that certain
packages are found at the top level (e.g.\ \f[CR]ripgrep\f[R]), and
other packages are found under package sets
(e.g.\ \f[CR]python310Packages.pip\f[R]).
We call this location within the catalog the \(lqpkg\-path\(rq.
.PP
The pkg\-path is searched when you execute a \f[CR]flox search\f[R]
command.
The pkg\-path is what\(cqs shown by \f[CR]flox show\f[R].
Finally, the pkg\-path appears in your manifest after a
\f[CR]flox install\f[R].
.IP
.EX
\f[B][install]\f[R]
ripgrep.pkg\-path = \(dqripgrep\(dq
pip.pkg\-path = \(dqpython310Packages.pip\(dq
.EE
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Install Options
.TP
\f[CR]\-i\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-id\f[R]
The install ID of the package as it will appear in the manifest
.TP
\f[CR]<package>\f[R]
The pkg\-path of the package to install as shown by `flox search'.
Append \f[CR]\(at<version>\f[R] to specify a version requirement, or
\f[CR]\(ha<outputs>\f[R] to select which outputs to install.
Use \f[CR]\(ha..\f[R] to install all outputs, or a comma\-separated list
such as \f[CR]\(habin,man\f[R] to install specific outputs.
The version constraint (\f[CR]\(at\f[R]) and output specifier
(\f[CR]\(ha\f[R]) are mutually exclusive.
.RS
.PP
Alternatively, an arbitrary Nix flake installable, or store path may be
specified.
See \f[B]\f[CB]manifest.toml(5)\f[B]\f[R] for more details.
.RE
.SS Environment Options
If no environment is specified for an environment command, the
environment in the current directory or the active environment that was
last activated is used.
.TP
\f[CR]\-d\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-dir\f[R]
Path containing a .flox/ directory.
.TP
\f[CR]\-r\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-reference\f[R]
A FloxHub environment, specified in the form \f[CR]<owner>/<name>\f[R].
.TP
\f[CR]\-D\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-default\f[R]
Use your default environment (\f[CR]<your\-user>/default\f[R]).
When unauthenticated in an interactive context, you will be prompted to
log in.
In non\-interactive contexts (e.g., scripts or CI), this flag will fail
with an error when authentication is missing.
.SS General Options
.TP
\f[CR]\-h\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-help\f[R]
Prints help information.
.PP
The following options can be passed when running any \f[CR]flox\f[R]
subcommand but must be specified \f[I]before\f[R] the subcommand.
.TP
\f[CR]\-v\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-verbose\f[R]
Increase logging verbosity.
Invoke multiple times for increasing detail.
.TP
\f[CR]\-q\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-quiet\f[R]
Silence logs except for errors.
